Deployment hardening - what the shipped web-server configuration now refuses


1. SUMMARY

   Three things about a FreeMED deployment are now enforced by the shipped
   configuration rather than left to the application:

     * The trees lib/, data/, tests/ and scripts/ are not web-readable. A
       request for any file in them is 403 on Apache and on nginx, whatever the
       PHP handler would have done with it. (CWE-548 / CWE-219.)

     * PATH_INFO is scoped. AcceptPathInfo stays On for the install root, because
       five entrypoints route on the trailing path, but every OTHER script that
       is addressed with a trailing path is refused with 403, so a file sink can
       only be reached through an entrypoint that is supposed to read
       PATH_INFO. (CWE-22 sibling - the class the 0.9.x help.php/chtml.php
       findings came from.)

     * register_argc_argv is Off in the runtime ini, so a query string can never
       look like a command line to a script that guards on $_SERVER['argc'] /
       $_SERVER['argv'] and mask a request as an empty 200. (CWE-16.)

   None of it changes a documented URL. The behaviour changes are the three
   undocumented trailing-path shapes in section 4, plus the one served URL that
   section 8 discloses: files under data/documents/unfiled/ (.djvu uploads)
   answered 200 at HEAD and are now 403. Every entrypoint, every static asset
   and every relay call answers exactly as it did before, and the tree-by-tree
   measurement is in tests/security/evidence/deployment-hardening.txt.


2. WHAT SHIPS, AND WHERE IT LANDS

     doc/freemed.apache.conf     installed by doc/Makefile as
                                 /etc/apache2/conf.d/freemed.conf
     docker/nginx.conf           the container's /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
     docker/php-custom.ini       mounted as a PHP conf.d drop-in by
                                 docker/docker-compose.yml
     the .htaccess files         installed by each subdirectory's Makefile
                                 (data, lib, scripts); they are the second
                                 layer, not the load-bearing one

   An operator on the packaged layout has nothing to do: doc/Makefile installs
   the Apache file and the .htaccess files, and the file aliases the application
   at /freemed exactly as before. An operator who serves the tree from their own
   vhost must copy the four <Directory> blocks (Apache) or the deny location
   (nginx) into it; nothing here depends on the distro default vhost.

   Note on the doc/ names, because it is the kind of thing that gets "fixed"
   wrongly: doc/Makefile's rule is `cp -rvf [A-Z]*`, a glob that keys on the
   FIRST CHARACTER only, so an uppercase-named file ships whatever its extension
   is (`VCALENDAR_AUTHENTICATION.md` would be installed exactly as
   `VCALENDAR_AUTHENTICATION` is - measured, see the transcript). What the rule
   does exclude is a lowercase name. These operator notes are uppercase and
   extensionless to match the rest of the directory, not to satisfy the glob;
   doc/freemed.apache.conf is lowercase and is installed by its own explicit
   `cp` line further down that Makefile.


3. WHY THE RULE LIVES IN THE SERVER CONFIGURATION, NOT IN .htaccess

   Four measured reasons, all of them from this tree at HEAD:

     * nginx reads no .htaccess at all, so on that layout the shipped
       per-directory rules do not exist. Measured there: data/schema/mysql/
       patient.sql (14559 bytes), tests/security/repro-relay-sqli.sh (30256
       bytes), tests/security/evidence/relay-allowlist.txt (23672 bytes) and
       scripts/security-sql-concat-check.sh (3107 bytes) all served as SOURCE,
       HTTP 200; lib/org/freemedsoftware/acl/.htaccess and
       data/store/.htaccess served as source; lib/API.php and lib/settings.php
       executed (200, empty body).

     * lib/ has no .htaccess to begin with. The repository has none and
       lib/Makefile installs only *.php plus the subdirectories, so the
       installed layout has none either: GET /lib/API.php was 200 (mod_php
       executed it) and GET /lib/perl/Config/IniFiles.pm was 200 with 70159
       bytes of Perl source.

     * The .htaccess files that existed were partly defeating each other. Four
       of them contained `<Files *> deny from * </Files>`, and `*` is not the
       `all` keyword a Deny takes - it matches no client, so the rule allowed
       everything it was meant to deny (GET /scripts/*.sh returned the script's
       source, 200). In data/store/ it did worse than nothing: the parent
       data/.htaccess denies the whole data/ tree (measured: /data/README 403),
       yet GET /data/store/no-image.png answered 200 with 60521 bytes, because
       the malformed Deny in the child took that subtree out of the inherited
       denial. All four are rewritten in this change.

     * Five more .htaccess files in the same trees - data/, data/schema/,
       data/config/, data/documents/unfiled/ and tests/ - carried 2.2-only
       syntax (`order`/`deny`), which is not merely legacy: on an Apache 2.4
       vhost that does not load mod_access_compat the file is a configuration
       error during URI mapping, so the tree answers 500 rather than the 403 the
       rule means. Measured, not inferred: a probe config loading the same
       modules minus mod_access_compat rejects all five as they were
       (`Invalid command 'order'`), and accepts them once they carry the same
       guarded block as the four above. Fix round 1 converted them, including
       data/documents/unfiled/ - whose `<Files *.djvu> allow from all` exception
       is discussed in section 8 - and left the vendored
       lib/org/freemedsoftware/acl/.htaccess alone (phpGACL is not this tree's
       file, and its own rule is unchanged).

     * AllowOverride. A vhost with `AllowOverride None` ignores every .htaccess
       file in this tree. The shipped file does set `AllowOverride All`, and
       with it the corrected .htaccess files do deny on their own (measured: with
       the server-configuration rule removed again, the rewritten scripts/
       .htaccess alone answers 403 for /scripts/*.sh) - but the rule must not
       depend on an operator leaving that setting in place, and the
       server-configuration rule does not.


4. THE PATH_INFO SCOPE

   AcceptPathInfo is On for the install root (the packaged file has set that
   since 0.9.x). These are the entrypoints that actually route on the trailing
   path, and they are the only ones that may have one:

     help.php         /help.php/<ui>/<locale>/<page>
     chtml.php        /chtml.php/<archive>
     relay.php        /relay.php/<provider>/<method>       (the UI transport)
     relay-gwt.php    /relay-gwt.php/<service>             (the GWT transport)
     controller.php   /controller.php/<ui>/<page>          (the Dojo UI: the
                      login redirect and the mainframe URL are built this way,
                      ui/dojo/load.php and ui/dojo/view/*.login.tpl)

   Anything else with a `.php/<path>` is 403 (Apache: a mod_rewrite [F] inside
   the install-root <Directory>; nginx: a `map` consulted by the PHP location).
   install.php also reads PATH_INFO, but only to strip it out of REQUEST_URI for
   base_uri - it does not route, and it is deliberately not in the list.

   The allowlist is ANCHORED to the request root, and that is load-bearing.
   Task 4.1 shipped it unanchored on both servers - `/(help|chtml|relay|
   relay-gwt|controller)\.php/` as a negated Apache RewriteCond on
   REQUEST_URI, and the same pattern as the first (winning) `map` entry - so it
   was a substring test: any request whose path contained an allowlisted
   `name.php/` ANYWHERE escaped the refusal and kept running the real script
   before it with PATH_INFO, which is exactly the class this section exists to
   refuse. Measured before the anchor and after it, on both servers:

     /install.php/x/help.php/              200 (123 bytes)   ->  403
     /install.php/x/relay.php/             200 (123 bytes)   ->  403
     /index.php/help.php/x                 302                ->  403
     /index.php/x/chtml.php/y              302                ->  403
     /vcalendar.php/x/controller.php/      401                ->  403
     /services/RemittCallback.php/x/help.php/  200 (2257)     ->  403

   The two mechanisms are anchored the same way but not in the same words.
   Apache matches the rule pattern in per-directory context, so the path it
   tests is relative to the application root whatever URL prefix the tree is
   mounted under (within `/freemed`, from a document root, or under any other
   Alias - all three measured, the last by adding a second Alias to the shipped
   configuration). nginx has no per-directory context to exploit, so its
   pattern names the prefix: `~^/(freemed/)?(help|chtml|relay|relay-gwt|
   controller)\.php/`. An installation served under a different prefix must add
   that prefix to the `map` entry, the same way it must add its own location
   block.

   Adding an entrypoint means editing two places and this list: the
   RewriteRule pattern in doc/freemed.apache.conf and the first `map` entry in
   docker/nginx.conf. A wrong entry here breaks whatever the entrypoint does
   with the path, so add one only for a script that reads
   $_SERVER['PATH_INFO'] itself.

   This is a behaviour change for exactly three shapes, measured before and
   after: /install.php/<path> was 200 ("FreeMED is already installed!") and is
   now 403; /vcalendar.php/<path> was 401, now 403; and
   /services/RemittCallback.php/<path> was 200, now 403. None of the three reads
   PATH_INFO, the plain URLs are unaffected, and the form that does matter -
   /services/RemittCallback.php?wsdl - is unchanged (200, verified).


5. register_argc_argv = Off

   PHP's compiled default for this setting is On, so without the line below a
   request that carries a query string looks like a command line to any script
   that guards on $_SERVER['argc'] / $_SERVER['argv']: the request halts with an
   empty 200 instead of running. That masking is what made the old vCalendar CLI
   guard swallow every query-string request (vcalendar.php now tests PHP_SAPI
   instead, which no ini setting can move), and it is the trap for anything else
   that copies the idiom.

   PROVENANCE OF THE MEASUREMENT, stated exactly because the setting is a
   guarantee only where the file is read. The ini behaviour below was measured
   on the VERIFICATION image (freemed-verify-php:apache, built FROM php:8.3-
   apache in this project's throwaway harness, drop-ins read from
   /usr/local/etc/php/conf.d/), NOT on the image this project's
   docker/docker-compose.yml ships, which is jbuchbinder/moodle-php-8.3-fpm
   (Bitnami layout) and which was not available to inspect. What that means for
   the shipped container, precisely: the drop-in mount
   (/opt/bitnami/php/etc/conf.d/custom.ini) predates this change and carries
   settings the deployment has been using (memory_limit, max_input_vars), so the
   mechanism is in use - but a Bitnami or php.ini-production install normally
   loads a php.ini that already sets register_argc_argv = Off, so on the shipped
   image the directive may be the second statement of the same fact rather than
   the only one. The explicit `Off` is still the right line to ship: wherever
   the file is read it wins, and it is the half of the pair that is verifiable.

   Measured with this exact docker/php-custom.ini mounted as a conf.d drop-in in
   that verification image, php 8.3.35, web SAPI (cli-server), request
   /argv.php?physician=1: with no ini mounted, register_argc_argv=On, argc=1,
   argv=["physician=1"] and the old guard halts; with this file mounted,
   register_argc_argv=Off (ini_get returns the empty string for that value),
   argc/argv unset and the old guard runs. In the same image, with no php.ini at
   all, `php --ini` reports "Loaded Configuration File: (none)" and
   php.ini-development / php.ini-production sit unused next to it - that is the
   image the number comes from.

   The setting is effective for the web SAPIs (mod_php, php-fpm, cli-server).
   The CLI SAPI reports it On whatever the ini says (measured with this file
   loaded: SAPI=cli, register_argc_argv='1', $argv set), so a command-line
   script must not use this setting as a CLI detector - test PHP_SAPI.

   A site that runs its own php.ini should add the same line to it; the
   docker/php-custom.ini file only covers the container deployment.


6. lib/settings.php - THE MEASUREMENT AND THE VERDICT

   lib/settings.php is generated by install.php and, in the classic install,
   lives under the document root. It defines DB_HOST, DB_NAME, DB_USER and
   DB_PASSWORD in the clear. Requested directly at HEAD:

     Apache 2.4 + mod_php:  GET /lib/settings.php -> 200, body 0 bytes
     nginx + php-fpm:       GET /lib/settings.php -> 200, body 0 bytes

   The source was NOT disclosed: the file is 1459 bytes and contains the
   DB_PASSWORD assignment, so a 0-byte body is the script executing and printing
   nothing, not the text. The finding is therefore bounded but real: an
   unauthenticated entrypoint existed to a file that must never be one, and the
   application, not the server, was the only thing standing between it and the
   credentials. Any layout where the PHP handler does not cover the path - a
   static file server, a proxy that serves .php as text, a vhost with the
   handler removed, a php-fpm pool with a narrower security.limit_extensions, a
   backup or a directory listing that exports it - returns the credentials to
   whoever asks (the chtml.php/help.php PATH_INFO class of bug is the same shape
   and was demonstrated in this project). This is why the deny rule is a
   server-configuration rule and not a note in the install guide.

   After this change the request is 403 on both servers, and so is every other
   file in lib/ and data/. If a site still gets the source of this file over
   HTTP, that is a server misconfiguration to fix before anything else - the
   .htaccess and <Directory> rules here are the belt, not the braces.


7. HOW TO VERIFY ON YOUR OWN SERVER

   Every request below must be refused (403) - adjust the prefix to your layout
   (the packaged one is /freemed):

     curl -sI https://host/freemed/lib/API.php
     curl -sI https://host/freemed/lib/settings.php
     curl -sI https://host/freemed/data/schema/mysql/patient.sql
     curl -sI https://host/freemed/tests/security/repro-relay-sqli.sh
     curl -sI https://host/freemed/scripts/security-sql-concat-check.sh
     curl -sI 'https://host/freemed/lib%2FAPI.php'        # nginx decodes the %2F
     curl -sI 'https://host/freemed/help.php/../scripts/security-sql-concat-check.sh'

   And these must still work (this is the no-regression half):

     curl -sI  https://host/freemed/index.php                                  # 302
     curl -sI  https://host/freemed/help.php/ui/en_US/page                     # 401 (its own login gate)
     curl -sI  https://host/freemed/chtml.php/main                             # 200
     curl -s   'https://host/freemed/relay.php/json/org.freemedsoftware.public.Login.LoggedIn'  # 200
     curl -sI  https://host/freemed/services/RemittCallback.php?wsdl           # 200

   With Apache, `apachectl configtest` must say Syntax OK after installing
   doc/freemed.apache.conf, and the file needs mod_rewrite (guarded by
   <IfModule>, so a server without it still starts - the four deny blocks do not
   depend on it). With nginx, `nginx -t` before reloading.


8. WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO

     * It does not stop PHP from including these files. lib/, data/ and the rest
       are read by include/require/readfile from an entrypoint; that is
       unchanged and intended. The rule is about URLs.

     * It does not cover ui/dojo/htdocs/, which is deliberately world-readable
       static UI (its own .htaccess allows everything), nor the rest of the
       application tree.

     * It does not remove doc/ from the web. The operator notes - this file,
       doc/VCALENDAR_AUTHENTICATION, doc/RELAY_ALLOWLIST and the rest - are
       still served as text; they contain no credentials. A site whose policy
       forbids publishing them can deny /doc/ the same way the four trees above
       are denied.

     * It denies data/documents/unfiled/, which used to be an exception inside
       the denied tree: that directory's .htaccess said `<Files *.djvu> order
       allow,deny / allow from all`, so unfiled .djvu uploads were fetchable by
       URL (measured at HEAD: 200, 60521 bytes on both servers). That path is now
       403 with the rest of data/, and fix round 1 rewrote the .htaccess file to
       deny as well, so the two layers agree instead of contradicting each other
       (measured on the .htaccess layer alone, on the Apache docroot harness: a
       .djvu file in that directory was 200 before the rewrite and 403 after it,
       with every other path in the tree unchanged). Nothing in this tree
       references it as a URL - the document viewer fetches page images through
       the relay (<relay>/org.freemedsoftware.module.<module>.GetDocumentPage),
       not from the filesystem path - and serving PHI by guessable URL is what
       this change exists to stop. A site that needs a different behaviour for
       that directory should state an explicit exception in its server
       configuration (and restrict it by extension), not rely on the .htaccess.
